{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KYRcRoPdCnkTX52uZHyJzt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: judge-bias-contamination-research\n3\tdescription: LLM judge bias与benchmark contamination领域的实验设计共识、竞品定位、方法论趋势——12篇论文蒸馏\n4\ttype: skill\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## Benchmark 与指标共识\n8\t\n9\t- **Judge bias 研究**用 MT-Bench（4/12）+ Arena-Hard（3/12）。Contamination 研究用 GSM8K+MMLU+ARC-C+HellaSwag 四件套（3/12）。G-MFRM 需覆盖两组。**置信度 高**\n10\t- **Human reference** 是必要验证：zheng2023 用 3K expert + 3K crowd votes，UDA 用 100 条，CyclicJudge 用 panel mean 作 proxy。**置信度 高**\n11\t- **核心指标**：Inter-judge std/Var(X̄)↓（CyclicJudge,UDA）| Agreement/Pearson r with human↑（zheng2023,UDA）| Rater parameter→0（wang2025）| Detection rate/δ̂（ConStat）| Kendall τ↑（PSN-IRT）。G-MFRM 需同时报 θ-RMSE + Kendall τ + judge/contamination 指标。\n12\t\n13\t## 方法论趋势\n14\t\n15\t**上升**：(1) Psychometric models 迁移至 LLM（MFRM, 4PL IRT, DG-IRT）(2) Multi-judge panels + 统计方法处理 judge variance (3) Performance-based contamination 定义取代 information-flow（ConStat: r²=0.94）。**置信度 高**\n16\t\n17\t**消退**：Perplexity-based 污染检测易绕过（ConStat 89% vs Shi 21.7%）；单 judge 已不可接受。**置信度 中**\n18\t\n19\t## G-MFRM 竞品定位\n20\t\n21\t| 竞品 | Judge Bias | Contamination | 可解释 | G-MFRM 优势 |\n22\t|------|:-:|:-:|:-:|------|\n23\t| CyclicJudge | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ | 联合建模 |\n24\t| UDA | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | 可解释+contamination |\n25\t| DCR | ✗ | ✓ | 部分 | judge facet |\n26\t| ConStat | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | judge facet |\n27\t| PSN-IRT | ✗ | 间接 | ✓ | multi-facet+judge |\n28\t\n29\t**核心空白（0/12篇）**：无论文同时建模 judge severity 与 contamination 的 confounding。CyclicJudge 证明 judge bias 占 >94% variance 但忽略 contamination；DCR 调整 accuracy 但忽略 judge。**置信度 高**\n30\t\n31\t## 实验设计经验\n32\t\n33\t- **Judge panel**：5-10 judges from ≥3 families（CyclicJudge, UDA, wang2025）**置信度 中**\n34\t- **Position swap**：pairwise evaluation 标准操作（zheng2023）**置信度 高**\n35\t- **显著性检验**：Bootstrap + BH 校正（ConStat, preference_leakage）或 paired Wilcoxon **置信度 高**\n36\t- **Self-preference**：judge 自评偏高是 universal pattern（CyclicJudge: Qwen 自评第一但共识排最后；UDA: [-21%,+56%] deviation）——G-MFRM 的 d_j 应捕获 **置信度 高**\n37\t\n38\t## 共同局限 → G-MFRM 卖点\n39\t\n40\t1. Judge bias 和 contamination 分别处理（6/12）→ joint estimation\n41\t2. 无 confounding 分析（0/12）→ ρ(d_j, c_n·e_i) 实验\n42\t3. 小规模验证（wang2025: 30人, CyclicJudge: 5 judges）→ 更大规模\n43\t4. 闭源模型训练数据不透明（4/12）→ black-box compatible\n44\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3840f6f-5cda-4115-aa82-47bd4eaaa707","uuid":"47cfb053-15c7-419c-af38-73330fc4b03d","timestamp":"2026-05-22T04:59:38.301Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod43/skills/projects/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/distilled-research.skill.md","content":"---\nname: judge-bias-contamination-research\ndescription: LLM judge bias与benchmark contamination领域的实验设计共识、竞品定位、方法论趋势——12篇论文蒸馏\ntype: skill\n---\n\n## Benchmark 与指标共识\n\n- **Judge bias 研究**用 MT-Bench（4/12）+ Arena-Hard（3/12）。Contamination 研究用 GSM8K+MMLU+ARC-C+HellaSwag 四件套（3/12）。G-MFRM 需覆盖两组。**置信度 高**\n- **Human reference** 是必要验证：zheng2023 用 3K expert + 3K crowd votes，UDA 用 100 条，CyclicJudge 用 panel mean 作 proxy。**置信度 高**\n- **核心指标**：Inter-judge std/Var(X̄)↓（CyclicJudge,UDA）| Agreement/Pearson r with human↑（zheng2023,UDA）| Rater parameter→0（wang2025）| Detection rate/δ̂（ConStat）| Kendall τ↑（PSN-IRT）。G-MFRM 需同时报 θ-RMSE + Kendall τ + judge/contamination 指标。\n\n## 方法论趋势\n\n**上升**：(1) Psychometric models 迁移至 LLM（MFRM, 4PL IRT, DG-IRT）(2) Multi-judge panels + 统计方法处理 judge variance (3) Performance-based contamination 定义取代 information-flow（ConStat: r²=0.94）。**置信度 高**\n\n**消退**：Perplexity-based 污染检测易绕过（ConStat 89% vs Shi 21.7%）；单 judge 已不可接受。**置信度 中**\n\n## G-MFRM 竞品定位\n\n| 竞品 | Judge Bias | Contamination | 可解释 | G-MFRM 优势 |\n|------|:-:|:-:|:-:|------|\n| CyclicJudge | ✓ | ✗ | ✓ | 联合建模 |\n| UDA | ✓ | ✗ | ✗ | 可解释+contamination |\n| DCR | ✗ | ✓ | 部分 | judge facet |\n| ConStat | ✗ | ✓ | ✓ | judge facet |\n| PSN-IRT | ✗ | 间接 | ✓ | multi-facet+judge |\n\n**核心空白（0/12篇）**：无论文同时建模 judge severity 与 contamination 的 confounding。CyclicJudge 证明 judge bias 占 >94% variance 但忽略 contamination；DCR 调整 accuracy 但忽略 judge。**置信度 高**\n\n## 实验设计经验\n\n- **Judge panel**：5-10 judges from ≥3 families（CyclicJudge, UDA, wang2025）**置信度 中**\n- **Position swap**：pairwise evaluation 标准操作（zheng2023）**置信度 高**\n- **显著性检验**：Bootstrap + BH 校正（ConStat, preference_leakage）或 paired Wilcoxon **置信度 高**\n- **Self-preference**：judge 自评偏高是 universal pattern（CyclicJudge: Qwen 自评第一但共识排最后；UDA: [-21%,+56%] deviation）——G-MFRM 的 d_j 应捕获 **置信度 高**\n\n## 共同局限 → G-MFRM 卖点\n\n1. Judge bias 和 contamination 分别处理（6/12）→ joint estimation\n2. 无 confounding 分析（0/12）→ ρ(d_j, c_n·e_i) 实验\n3. 小规模验证（wang2025: 30人, CyclicJudge: 5 judges）→ 更大规模\n4. 闭源模型训练数据不透明（4/12）→ black-box compatible\n","numLines":44,"startLine":1,"totalLines":44}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PMPPRVRUrXvioVrK2vNTXW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Real contamination is negligible on current benchmarks — G-MFRM\n2\t  cannot improve rankings because there is nothing substantial to correct. Pivot to:\n3\t  (1) G-MFRM as principled diagnostic audit framework, (2) the null finding itself\n4\t  as contribution challenging contamination assumptions, (3) identifiability/methodology\n5\t  contribution independent of effect size. Target ARR Findings or AAAI evaluation\n6\t  workshop.'\n7\timagined_experiments:\n8\t- name: multi_judge_data_collection\n9\t  plan_id: exp-001\n10\t  description: Collect multi-judge evaluation data on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard with\n11\t    8 judges × 20+ target models\n12\t  expected_results:\n13\t    total_judges_collected: 8\n14\t    total_target_models: 22\n15\t    total_judgments_mtbench: 14080\n16\t    total_judgments_arenahard: 35200\n17\t    position_swap_agreement: 0.72\n18\t    inter_judge_agreement_fleiss_kappa: 0.38\n19\t    judge_severity_range_raw: 0.85\n20\t    missing_data_pct: 3.2\n21\t    api_cost_usd: 870\n22\t    gpu_hours_used: 118\n23\t  result_rationale: Data collection itself is scenario-independent — the judges produce\n24\t    scores regardless of whether contamination exists. However, under worst tier the\n25\t    inter-judge agreement is moderate (κ≈0.38) because judges disagree primarily on\n26\t    difficulty/style rather than contamination-driven inflation. Position swap agreement\n27\t    is typical (0.72) indicating normal positional bias. The raw judge severity range\n28\t    (0.85 logits) is present but moderate — some judges (e.g., Qwen3.6-27B) show compressed\n29\t    scoring ranges (<0.3 logit range), limiting MFRM's value for those facets.\n30\t  tables:\n31\t  - name: Judge Collection Summary\n32\t    columns:\n33\t    - Judge\n34\t    - N_judgments\n35\t    - Mean_score\n36\t    - SD\n37\t    - Severity_range\n38\t    - Position_bias\n39\t    rows:\n40\t    - - GPT-5.5\n41\t      - 4400\n42\t      - 7.82\n43\t      - 1.21\n44\t      - ref\n45\t      - 0.08\n46\t    - - Claude Opus 4.7\n47\t      - 4400\n48\t      - 7.45\n49\t      - 1.35\n50\t      - -0.37\n51\t      - 0.12\n52\t    - - Claude Sonnet 4.6\n53\t      - 4400\n54\t      - 7.61\n55\t      - 1.18\n56\t      - -0.21\n57\t      - 0.09\n58\t    - - DeepSeek-V4-Pro\n59\t      - 4400\n60\t      - 7.28\n61\t      - 1.42\n62\t      - -0.54\n63\t      - 0.15\n64\t    - - Qwen3-235B-A22B\n65\t      - 4400\n66\t      - 7.53\n67\t      - 1.08\n68\t      - -0.29\n69\t      - 0.11\n70\t    - - Qwen3.6-27B\n71\t      - 4400\n72\t      - 7.91\n73\t      - 0.82\n74\t      - 0.09\n75\t      - 0.06\n76\t    - - Llama 4 Maverick\n77\t      - 4400\n78\t      - 7.15\n79\t      - 1.48\n80\t      - -0.67\n81\t      - 0.14\n82\t    - - Gemini 2.5 Pro\n83\t      - 4400\n84\t      - 7.68\n85\t      - 1.25\n86\t      - -0.14\n87\t      - 0.1\n88\t  figures_description:\n89\t  - 'Score distribution heatmap (judges × models): relatively uniform inflation pattern\n90\t    across models — no clear contamination-driven ''spikes'' for specific model-item\n91\t    pairs'\n92\t  analysis: 'Data quality is adequate for MFRM fitting but the score distributions\n93\t    already hint at worst-tier outcome: no judge shows dramatically different severity\n94\t    for known-contaminated vs clean models. The compressed scoring range of Qwen3.6-27B\n95\t    (SD=0.82) will limit its contribution to facet separation.'\n96\t- name: item_exposure_estimation\n97\t  plan_id: exp-002\n98\t  description: Estimate per-item exposure e_i via three-source pipeline (performance\n99\t    gap, temporal, LogProber)\n100\t  expected_results:\n101\t    ei_mean: 0.18\n102\t    ei_median: 0.12\n103\t    ei_std: 0.22\n104\t    ei_max: 0.71\n105\t    ei_iqr:\n106\t    - 0.04\n107\t    - 0.28\n108\t    pct_items_ei_above_0.5: 8.5\n109\t    performance_gap_signal_snr: 1.2\n110\t    temporal_signal_coverage_pct: 35\n111\t    logprober_coverage_pct: 45\n112\t    source_intercorrelation_gap_temporal: 0.28\n113\t    source_intercorrelation_gap_logprober: 0.31\n114\t    source_intercorrelation_temporal_logprober: 0.19\n115\t    weighted_composite_internal_consistency_alpha: 0.24\n116\t    spline_correction_r2: 0.42\n117\t    reference_variant_quality_check_pass_pct: 78\n118\t  result_rationale: Under worst tier, e_i pipeline produces weak/noisy signal (effective\n119\t    σ_e ≈ 0.3–0.5 in the core assumption). Most items show near-zero exposure because\n120\t    current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) were designed to resist\n121\t    memorization. The three sources have low intercorrelation (0.19–0.31) indicating\n122\t    they capture different noise rather than converging on a true signal. Internal\n123\t    consistency α=0.24 is far below psychometric acceptability (0.70). The few items\n124\t    with e_i > 0.5 are likely legacy items from MT-Bench v1 that survived into the\n125\t    refresh. LogProber coverage is limited (45%) because many target models lack logprob\n126\t    APIs. The spline difficulty correction only explains 42% of variance in raw performance\n127\t    gaps — most of the gap is difficulty-driven, not contamination-driven.\n128\t  tables:\n129\t  - name: Exposure Source Contribution\n130\t    columns:\n131\t    - Source\n132\t    - Coverage_pct\n133\t    - Mean_signal\n134\t    - SD\n135\t    - SNR\n136\t    - Correlation_with_composite\n137\t    rows:\n138\t    - - Performance gap (primary)\n139\t      - 100\n140\t      - 0.15\n141\t      - 0.19\n142\t      - 1.2\n143\t      - 0.78\n144\t    - - Temporal signal\n145\t      - 35\n146\t      - 0.21\n147\t      - 0.31\n148\t      - 0.8\n149\t      - 0.52\n150\t    - - LogProber\n151\t      - 45\n152\t      - 0.14\n153\t      - 0.24\n154\t      - 0.9\n155\t      - 0.61\n156\t  - name: e_i Distribution by Benchmark\n157\t    columns:\n158\t    - Benchmark\n159\t    - N_items\n160\t    - Median_ei\n161\t    - Mean_ei\n162\t    - Pct_above_0.5\n163\t    - Max_ei\n164\t    rows:\n165\t    - - MT-Bench\n166\t      - 80\n167\t      - 0.14\n168\t      - 0.2\n169\t      - 10.0\n170\t      - 0.71\n171\t    - - Arena-Hard\n172\t      - 500\n173\t      - 0.11\n174\t      - 0.17\n175\t      - 7.8\n176\t      - 0.63\n177\t  figures_description:\n178\t  - 'e_i histogram: heavily right-skewed with mode near 0, long thin tail — most items\n179\t    effectively unexposed'\n180\t  - 'Source agreement scatter: three panels showing weak positive correlations between\n181\t    each pair of sources, large scatter'\n182\t  analysis: The e_i pipeline's internal consistency (α=0.24) is a critical weakness.\n183\t    The signal is real but noisy — identifiable in aggregate but unreliable at per-item\n184\t    level. This means Stage 3 fitting will have limited power to separate c_n from\n185\t    noise in e_i. The low median (0.12) confirms that most benchmark items are effectively\n186\t    clean, leaving little contamination signal for G-MFRM to exploit.\n187\t- name: gmfrm_real_data_fit\n188\t  plan_id: exp-003\n189\t  description: Fit G-MFRM three-stage pipeline on real data; compare fair scores with\n190\t    Chatbot Arena (Kendall-τ)\n191\t  expected_results:\n192\t    stage1_mfrm_convergence_rhat_max: 1.02\n193\t    stage1_mfrm_ess_min: 312\n194\t    stage3_joint_convergence_rhat_max: 1.12\n195\t    stage3_joint_ess_min: 58\n196\t    stage3_cn_rhat_max: 1.18\n197\t    stage3_cn_ess_min: 34\n198\t    kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_mtbench: 0.76\n199\t    kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_arenahard: 0.78\n200\t    kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_mtbench: 0.75\n201\t    kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_arenahard: 0.77\n202\t    cn_median_fitted: 0.15\n203\t    cn_mean_fitted: 0.22\n204\t    cn_max_fitted: 0.61\n205\t    cn_95ci_width_median: 0.48\n206\t    theta_fair_vs_theta_mfrm_rank_correlation: 0.97\n207\t    pct_models_rank_change_geq_2: 9.1\n208\t    divergent_transitions_pct: 2.8\n209\t    runtime_hours: 8.5\n210\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, real c_n distribution has median < 0.3 (fitted\n211\t    median=0.15). Most models have near-zero contamination susceptibility — the c_n\n212\t    posterior piles up near the half-Cauchy prior''s mode. The few models with c_n\n213\t    > 0.4 have wide credible intervals (median CI width=0.48) because e_i signal is\n214\t    weak. Stage 3 convergence is problematic: R̂=1.12 overall and up to 1.18 for c_n\n215\t    parameters specifically, with ESS as low as 34 for some c_n. This is because the\n216\t    data provides minimal information about c_n when e_i is near-zero for most items\n217\t    — the likelihood surface is nearly flat in the c_n direction. The resulting fair\n218\t    scores θ̂ barely differ from MFRM-only θ̂ (rank correlation 0.97), meaning the\n219\t    contamination correction has negligible practical effect. Kendall-τ with Arena\n220\t    is 0.76–0.78, within the expected range for this tier but not meaningfully better\n221\t    than MFRM-only.'\n222\t  tables:\n223\t  - name: G-MFRM Fit Summary by Benchmark\n224\t    columns:\n225\t    - Benchmark\n226\t    - R_hat_max\n227\t    - ESS_min\n228\t    - Divergent_pct\n229\t    - cn_median\n230\t    - cn_max\n231\t    - tau_Arena\n232\t    rows:\n233\t    - - MT-Bench\n234\t      - 1.12\n235\t      - 58\n236\t      - 2.8\n237\t      - 0.17\n238\t      - 0.61\n239\t      - 0.76\n240\t    - - Arena-Hard\n241\t      - 1.08\n242\t      - 72\n243\t      - 1.5\n244\t      - 0.14\n245\t      - 0.53\n246\t      - 0.78\n247\t  - name: Fitted c_n Distribution (Top 5 Models)\n248\t    columns:\n249\t    - Model\n250\t    - c_n_mean\n251\t    - c_n_95CI_low\n252\t    - c_n_95CI_high\n253\t    - CI_width\n254\t    - R_hat\n255\t    rows:\n256\t    - - Model_A (suspected legacy)\n257\t      - 0.61\n258\t      - 0.18\n259\t      - 1.12\n260\t      - 0.94\n261\t      - 1.15\n262\t    - - Model_B\n263\t      - 0.43\n264\t      - 0.08\n265\t      - 0.89\n266\t      - 0.81\n267\t      - 1.18\n268\t    - - Model_C\n269\t      - 0.35\n270\t      - 0.05\n271\t      - 0.74\n272\t      - 0.69\n273\t      - 1.11\n274\t    - - Model_D\n275\t      - 0.22\n276\t      - 0.01\n277\t      - 0.58\n278\t      - 0.57\n279\t      - 1.09\n280\t    - - Model_E\n281\t      - 0.19\n282\t      - 0.01\n283\t      - 0.52\n284\t      - 0.51\n285\t      - 1.07\n286\t  figures_description:\n287\t  - 'c_n posterior density plot: most posteriors overlap heavily with zero; only 2-3\n288\t    models have posterior mass clearly above 0.3'\n289\t  - 'θ_fair vs θ_mfrm scatter: nearly perfect diagonal, with 2-3 outliers shifted\n290\t    slightly downward (the high-c_n models)'\n291\t  - 'Trace plots for c_n parameters: visible autocorrelation, slow mixing for models\n292\t    with c_n near boundary'\n293\t  analysis: 'Stage 1 MFRM fits well (R̂=1.02) — judge severity and item difficulty\n294\t    are well-identified. But Stage 3 struggles with c_n: the combination of weak e_i\n295\t    signal and low true contamination means the data cannot distinguish c_n=0 from\n296\t    c_n=0.3 for most models. The 2.8% divergent transitions on MT-Bench indicate the\n297\t    posterior geometry is problematic near the c_n≈0 boundary. Practically, fair scores\n298\t    θ̂ are indistinguishable from MFRM-only estimates for 20/22 models. The G-MFRM\n299\t    ''correction'' affects only 2-3 models with possible legacy contamination, and\n300\t    even those corrections are within posterior uncertainty.'\n301\t- name: baseline_comparison\n302\t  plan_id: exp-004\n303\t  description: Compare G-MFRM against 8 baselines; Kendall-τ and Spearman-ρ with Chatbot\n304\t    Arena; rank change analysis\n305\t  expected_results:\n306\t    kendall_tau_raw_average: 0.74\n307\t    kendall_tau_mfrm_only: 0.77\n308\t    kendall_tau_sequential: 0.76\n309\t    kendall_tau_cyclicjudge: 0.78\n310\t    kendall_tau_cyclicjudge_discard: 0.77\n311\t    kendall_tau_dcr: 0.75\n312\t    kendall_tau_uda: 0.79\n313\t    kendall_tau_prefleak_calib: 0.76\n314\t    kendall_tau_gmfrm: 0.77\n315\t    best_baseline: UDA\n316\t    delta_tau_vs_best_baseline: -0.02\n317\t    delta_tau_vs_mfrm_only: 0.0\n318\t    wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_uda: 0.34\n319\t    wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge: 0.62\n320\t    spearman_rho_gmfrm: 0.89\n321\t    spearman_rho_best_baseline: 0.91\n322\t    rank_change_models_geq2_positions: 2\n323\t    top5_stability_gmfrm: 0.8\n324\t    top5_stability_best_baseline: 0.8\n325\t    post_hoc_power_for_observed_delta: 0.12\n326\t    n_models: 22\n327\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, Δτ is in [-0.02, 0.01] range. G-MFRM (τ=0.77)\n328\t    is actually slightly BELOW UDA (τ=0.79) — UDA''s neural K-factor adjustment captures\n329\t    judge-specific biases that are the dominant source of ranking noise when contamination\n330\t    is negligible. CyclicJudge (τ=0.78) also edges out G-MFRM because round-robin\n331\t    bias elimination is effective when contamination is near-zero. The Wilcoxon test\n332\t    is massively underpowered (power=0.12 at N=22 models for detecting Δτ=0.02) —\n333\t    we cannot distinguish methods statistically. G-MFRM ties MFRM-only (both τ=0.77),\n334\t    confirming the contamination correction adds nothing when there''s nothing to\n335\t    correct. Rank changes are minimal: only 2 models shift ≥2 positions between G-MFRM\n336\t    and best baseline, and these are within posterior uncertainty. This is the fatal\n337\t    result for Claim 3.'\n338\t  tables:\n339\t  - name: Main Comparison Table\n340\t    columns:\n341\t    - Method\n342\t    - Kendall_tau\n343\t    - Spearman_rho\n344\t    - Rank_changes_geq2\n345\t    - Top5_stable\n346\t    rows:\n347\t    - - Raw multi-judge average\n348\t      - 0.74\n349\t      - 0.86\n350\t      - ref\n351\t      - ref\n352\t    - - MFRM-only (Stage 1)\n353\t      - 0.77\n354\t      - 0.89\n355\t      - 3\n356\t      - 4/5\n357\t    - - Sequential (MFRM→contamination)\n358\t      - 0.76\n359\t      - 0.88\n360\t      - 2\n361\t      - 4/5\n362\t    - - CyclicJudge\n363\t      - 0.78\n364\t      - 0.9\n365\t      - 3\n366\t      - 4/5\n367\t    - - CyclicJudge + item discard\n368\t      - 0.77\n369\t      - 0.89\n370\t      - 2\n371\t      - 4/5\n372\t    - - DCR Factor\n373\t      - 0.75\n374\t      - 0.87\n375\t      - 2\n376\t      - 4/5\n377\t    - - UDA\n378\t      - 0.79\n379\t      - 0.91\n380\t      - 4\n381\t      - 4/5\n382\t    - - Preference Leakage calibration\n383\t      - 0.76\n384\t      - 0.88\n385\t      - 2\n386\t      - 4/5\n387\t    - - G-MFRM (ours)\n388\t      - 0.77\n389\t      - 0.89\n390\t      - 2\n391\t      - 4/5\n392\t  - name: Statistical Tests\n393\t    columns:\n394\t    - Comparison\n395\t    - Delta_tau\n396\t    - Wilcoxon_p\n397\t    - Power_at_alpha_0.05\n398\t    - Significant?\n399\t    rows:\n400\t    - - G-MFRM vs UDA\n401\t      - -0.02\n402\t      - 0.34\n403\t      - 0.12\n404\t      - 'No'\n405\t    - - G-MFRM vs CyclicJudge\n406\t      - -0.01\n407\t      - 0.62\n408\t      - 0.08\n409\t      - 'No'\n410\t    - - G-MFRM vs MFRM-only\n411\t      - 0.0\n412\t      - 0.91\n413\t      - 0.05\n414\t      - 'No'\n415\t    - - G-MFRM vs Sequential\n416\t      - 0.01\n417\t      - 0.55\n418\t      - 0.09\n419\t      - 'No'\n420\t    - - G-MFRM vs Raw average\n421\t      - 0.03\n422\t      - 0.18\n423\t      - 0.22\n424\t      - 'No'\n425\t  figures_description:\n426\t  - 'Bootstrap confidence interval plot: all methods'' τ CIs overlap heavily; G-MFRM\n427\t    CI centered at 0.77 with [0.71, 0.83] — indistinguishable from 5 other methods'\n428\t  - 'Rank change alluvial diagram: nearly static rankings across methods; 2-3 models\n429\t    shift 1-2 positions, driven by judge bias correction not contamination'\n430\t  analysis: 'Claim 3 is dead. G-MFRM does not improve over best baselines — it slightly\n431\t    underperforms UDA. Post-hoc power analysis (power=0.12) confirms the study is\n432\t    fundamentally underpowered to detect the observed effect size with N=22 models.\n433\t    Even with 50 models, power would be ~0.25 for Δτ=0.02. The ranking improvement\n434\t    is non-significant by any standard. MFRM-only and G-MFRM produce identical τ,\n435\t    confirming the contamination gating mechanism adds no signal. Pivot narrative:\n436\t    G-MFRM ''degrades gracefully'' to MFRM level when contamination is absent — this\n437\t    is a feature, not a failure.'\n438\t- name: contamination_magnitude_calibration\n439\t  plan_id: exp-005\n440\t  description: Estimate real contamination magnitude; determine if c_n matches mvp_001\n441\t    (weak) or mvp_002 (strong)\n442\t  expected_results:\n443\t    real_cn_median: 0.15\n444\t    real_cn_mean: 0.22\n445\t    real_cn_max: 0.61\n446\t    real_cn_iqr:\n447\t    - 0.04\n448\t    - 0.32\n449\t    pct_models_cn_above_0.5: 9.1\n450\t    pct_models_cn_above_1.0: 0.0\n451\t    closest_mvp_match: below mvp_001\n452\t    mvp_001_cn_mean_was: 0.5\n453\t    ratio_real_to_mvp001: 0.44\n454\t    constat_delta_correlation_with_cn: 0.32\n455\t    constat_delta_median: 0.08\n456\t    cn_distribution_shape: heavily right-skewed, mode near 0\n457\t    bimodality_coefficient: 0.31\n458\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, real c_n median is in [0.05, 0.3] — we get\n459\t    0.15, which is BELOW even mvp_001''s assumption of c~0.5. The distribution is\n460\t    not bimodal but heavily right-skewed with mode near zero: most models have negligible\n461\t    contamination, 2-3 have moderate (0.4-0.6), none reach 1.0. This is well below\n462\t    the regime where G-MFRM''s gating mechanism has meaningful leverage. The ConStat\n463\t    per-model delta validates only weakly (r=0.32) because both signals are near-floor\n464\t    — correlation is attenuated by floor effects and measurement noise. The finding\n465\t    itself IS informative: current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2)\n466\t    appear to have effectively low contamination. This challenges assumptions in contamination\n467\t    literature that training data overlap produces large performance inflation on\n468\t    these specific benchmarks.'\n469\t  tables:\n470\t  - name: Contamination Magnitude Comparison\n471\t    columns:\n472\t    - Source\n473\t    - cn_median\n474\t    - cn_mean\n475\t    - cn_max\n476\t    - Interpretation\n477\t    rows:\n478\t    - - MVP synthetic (mvp_001)\n479\t      - 0.5\n480\t      - 0.5\n481\t      - 1.0\n482\t      - Assumed moderate\n483\t    - - MVP synthetic (mvp_002/003)\n484\t      - 1.5\n485\t      - 1.5\n486\t      - 2.5\n487\t      - Assumed strong\n488\t    - - Real data (this experiment)\n489\t      - 0.15\n490\t      - 0.22\n491\t      - 0.61\n492\t      - Negligible to weak\n493\t  - name: Per-Model c_n Estimates\n494\t    columns:\n495\t    - Model\n496\t    - c_n_posterior_mean\n497\t    - 95CI\n498\t    - ConStat_delta\n499\t    - Category\n500\t    rows:\n501\t    - - GPT-5.5\n502\t      - 0.08\n503\t      - '[0.01, 0.29]'\n504\t      - 0.05\n505\t      - negligible\n506\t    - - Claude Opus 4.7\n507\t      - 0.05\n508\t      - '[0.00, 0.21]'\n509\t      - 0.03\n510\t      - negligible\n511\t    - - Llama 4 405B\n512\t      - 0.35\n513\t      - '[0.06, 0.72]'\n514\t      - 0.15\n515\t      - weak\n516\t    - - Qwen3-235B\n517\t      - 0.43\n518\t      - '[0.08, 0.89]'\n519\t      - 0.18\n520\t      - weak-moderate\n521\t    - - DeepSeek-V4\n522\t      - 0.61\n523\t      - '[0.18, 1.12]'\n524\t      - 0.22\n525\t      - moderate (max)\n526\t  figures_description:\n527\t  - 'c_n distribution: kernel density showing heavy pile-up near 0, thin tail to 0.6;\n528\t    overlaid with mvp_001 and mvp_002 assumed distributions for visual comparison\n529\t    of how much weaker real signal is'\n530\t  - 'ConStat delta vs G-MFRM c_n scatter: weak positive trend (r=0.32), large scatter,\n531\t    most points clustered near origin'\n532\t  analysis: 'This is the definitive worst-tier signal. Real contamination is far below\n533\t    synthetic assumptions. The mvp_002/003 success (43.8% RMSE reduction) required\n534\t    c_n~1.5 — real data has c_n~0.15 (10× weaker). Even mvp_001 assumed c~0.5. The\n535\t    G-MFRM''s gating mechanism is solving a problem that barely exists on these benchmarks.\n536\t    Pivot implications: (1) the null finding about contamination magnitude is itself\n537\t    publishable — benchmarks may be cleaner than assumed; (2) G-MFRM''s value is as\n538\t    a diagnostic tool that CONFIRMS low contamination rather than correcting for high\n539\t    contamination; (3) need to find or construct a benchmark with known high contamination\n540\t    for validation case.'\n541\t- name: judge_count_sensitivity\n542\t  plan_id: exp-006\n543\t  description: Vary judges (2,4,6,8); measure G-MFRM degradation; leave-one-judge-out\n544\t    residual diagnostic\n545\t  expected_results:\n546\t    tau_8_judges: 0.77\n547\t    tau_6_judges: 0.76\n548\t    tau_4_judges: 0.75\n549\t    tau_2_judges: 0.73\n550\t    tau_mfrm_only_8_judges: 0.77\n551\t    tau_mfrm_only_4_judges: 0.75\n552\t    delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_8j: 0.0\n553\t    delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_6j: 0.0\n554\t    delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_4j: 0.0\n555\t    delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_2j: -0.01\n556\t    cn_identifiability_threshold_judges: not reached\n557\t    leave_one_out_max_residual_change: 0.04\n558\t    leave_one_out_same_family_bias_detected: false\n559\t    convergence_2j_rhat_max: 1.25\n560\t    convergence_4j_rhat_max: 1.15\n561\t    convergence_6j_rhat_max: 1.1\n562\t  result_rationale: Under worst tier, G-MFRM never outperforms MFRM-only at any judge\n563\t    count because contamination is negligible — the gating mechanism adds parameters\n564\t    without adding signal. At 2 judges, G-MFRM actually slightly underperforms MFRM-only\n565\t    (Δτ=-0.01) due to over-parameterization with insufficient data. Convergence degrades\n566\t    progressively as judges decrease (R̂=1.25 at 2 judges) because c_n parameters\n567\t    are completely unidentifiable with sparse judge coverage and weak e_i. The leave-one-judge-out\n568\t    diagnostic shows no systematic same-family bias pattern — residual changes are\n569\t    small (max 0.04) because contamination effects are too weak to create detectable\n570\t    judge-model interactions. The practical conclusion is that with ≥4 judges, G-MFRM\n571\t    is harmless (degrades gracefully to MFRM level) but never beneficial for ranking.\n572\t  tables:\n573\t  - name: Judge Count Sensitivity\n574\t    columns:\n575\t    - N_judges\n576\t    - tau_GMFRM\n577\t    - tau_MFRM\n578\t    - Delta\n579\t    - R_hat_max\n580\t    - ESS_min_cn\n581\t    rows:\n582\t    - - 8\n583\t      - 0.77\n584\t      - 0.77\n585\t      - 0.0\n586\t      - 1.12\n587\t      - 58\n588\t    - - 6\n589\t      - 0.76\n590\t      - 0.76\n591\t      - 0.0\n592\t      - 1.1\n593\t      - 65\n594\t    - - 4\n595\t      - 0.75\n596\t      - 0.75\n597\t      - 0.0\n598\t      - 1.15\n599\t      - 42\n600\t    - - 2\n601\t      - 0.73\n602\t      - 0.74\n603\t      - -0.01\n604\t      - 1.25\n605\t      - 22\n606\t  - name: Leave-One-Judge-Out Residual Diagnostic\n607\t    columns:\n608\t    - Removed_judge\n609\t    - Family_overlap?\n610\t    - Max_residual_change\n611\t    - Systematic_bias?\n612\t    rows:\n613\t    - - GPT-5.5\n614\t      - Yes (GPT family targets)\n615\t      - 0.04\n616\t      - 'No'\n617\t    - - Claude Opus 4.7\n618\t      - Yes (Claude targets)\n619\t      - 0.03\n620\t      - 'No'\n621\t    - - DeepSeek-V4-Pro\n622\t      - Yes (DeepSeek targets)\n623\t      - 0.02\n624\t      - 'No'\n625\t    - - Qwen3-235B-A22B\n626\t      - Yes (Qwen targets)\n627\t      - 0.03\n628\t      - 'No'\n629\t    - - Llama 4 Maverick\n630\t      - Yes (Llama targets)\n631\t      - 0.02\n632\t      - 'No'\n633\t  figures_description:\n634\t  - 'Performance degradation curve: two nearly overlapping lines (G-MFRM and MFRM-only)\n635\t    both declining gently from 8→2 judges; gap is zero or negative throughout'\n636\t  - 'R̂ vs judge count: exponential increase below 4 judges for c_n parameters specifically,\n637\t    while θ and d parameters remain well-behaved'\n638\t  analysis: 'This experiment confirms G-MFRM adds nothing over MFRM-only at any judge\n639\t    count in the worst tier. The contamination facet is unidentifiable in practice.\n640\t    However, the graceful degradation finding is publishable as a safety property:\n641\t    G-MFRM doesn''t HARM rankings when contamination is absent. The leave-one-judge-out\n642\t    diagnostic is useful as a methodological contribution regardless of contamination\n643\t    magnitude — it provides a principled way to detect preference leakage without\n644\t    requiring contamination to be present.'\n645\t- name: exposure_indicator_ablation\n646\t  plan_id: exp-007\n647\t  description: 'Ablation: contribution of each exposure source to e_i quality; leave-one-out\n648\t    analysis'\n649\t  expected_results:\n650\t    full_pipeline_alpha: 0.24\n651\t    leave_out_performance_gap_alpha: 0.15\n652\t    leave_out_temporal_alpha: 0.22\n653\t    leave_out_logprober_alpha: 0.2\n654\t    performance_gap_unique_contribution: 0.09\n655\t    temporal_unique_contribution: 0.02\n656\t    logprober_unique_contribution: 0.04\n657\t    performance_gap_only_tau: 0.76\n658\t    temporal_only_tau: 0.75\n659\t    logprober_only_tau: 0.75\n660\t    full_pipeline_tau: 0.77\n661\t    no_ei_mfrm_only_tau: 0.77\n662\t    delta_any_source_vs_no_ei:\n663\t    - -0.02\n664\t    - 0.01\n665\t  result_rationale: Under worst tier, e_i pipeline has internal consistency α=0.24\n666\t    (floor). When we ablate sources, the full pipeline (α=0.24) is already so weak\n667\t    that removing any single source barely changes downstream τ. The performance gap\n668\t    is the strongest single source but still weak (unique contribution = 0.09 to α).\n669\t    Temporal and LogProber add negligible incremental signal. Critically, ALL configurations\n670\t    of e_i produce the same downstream τ (0.75–0.77) because the contamination correction\n671\t    itself is inconsequential — varying e_i quality doesn't matter when c_n ≈ 0. The\n672\t    'no e_i' condition (MFRM-only, τ=0.77) matches or beats all e_i conditions, confirming\n673\t    the entire exposure estimation pipeline is effort without payoff on these benchmarks.\n674\t  tables:\n675\t  - name: Exposure Source Ablation\n676\t    columns:\n677\t    - Configuration\n678\t    - Alpha\n679\t    - e_i_mean\n680\t    - e_i_SD\n681\t    - Downstream_tau\n682\t    - Delta_vs_MFRM_only\n683\t    rows:\n684\t    - - Full pipeline (3 sources)\n685\t      - 0.24\n686\t      - 0.18\n687\t      - 0.22\n688\t      - 0.77\n689\t      - 0.0\n690\t    - - Without performance gap\n691\t      - 0.15\n692\t      - 0.16\n693\t      - 0.2\n694\t      - 0.76\n695\t      - -0.01\n696\t    - - Without temporal\n697\t      - 0.22\n698\t      - 0.17\n699\t      - 0.21\n700\t      - 0.77\n701\t      - 0.0\n702\t    - - Without LogProber\n703\t      - 0.2\n704\t      - 0.19\n705\t      - 0.23\n706\t      - 0.76\n707\t      - -0.01\n708\t    - - Performance gap only\n709\t      - N/A\n710\t      - 0.15\n711\t      - 0.19\n712\t      - 0.76\n713\t      - -0.01\n714\t    - - No e_i (MFRM-only)\n715\t      - N/A\n716\t      - N/A\n717\t      - N/A\n718\t      - 0.77\n719\t      - ref\n720\t  figures_description:\n721\t  - 'Bar chart of unique variance contribution per source: all bars near zero, performance\n722\t    gap slightly taller but still below meaningful threshold'\n723\t  - 'Downstream τ forest plot: all configurations'' CIs overlap completely, including\n724\t    MFRM-only baseline'\n725\t  analysis: 'The ablation confirms that no e_i source meaningfully contributes to\n726\t    ranking quality under worst tier. This is not a pipeline failure per se — it''s\n727\t    that the signal being measured (item exposure) is genuinely near-zero on these\n728\t    benchmarks. Pivot narrative: the pipeline is VALIDATED for measuring exposure\n729\t    (internal structure exists, α=0.24 > 0), but the underlying construct is at floor.\n730\t    This supports the ''benchmarks are clean'' narrative contribution.'\n731\t- name: facet_fit_diagnostics\n732\t  plan_id: exp-008\n733\t  description: 'Measurement quality: infit/outfit, person-fit, divergent transitions,\n734\t    posterior predictive checks'\n735\t  expected_results:\n736\t    theta_infit_mean: 1.02\n737\t    theta_outfit_mean: 1.05\n738\t    theta_misfit_pct: 4.5\n739\t    b_infit_mean: 0.99\n740\t    b_outfit_mean: 1.03\n741\t    b_misfit_pct: 6.2\n742\t    d_infit_mean: 1.01\n743\t    d_outfit_mean: 1.04\n744\t    d_misfit_pct: 12.5\n745\t    cn_infit_mean: 1.15\n746\t    cn_outfit_mean: 1.28\n747\t    cn_misfit_pct: 36.4\n748\t    divergent_transitions_total: 142\n749\t    divergent_transitions_pct: 2.2\n750\t    posterior_predictive_p_global: 0.41\n751\t    posterior_predictive_p_items_below_0.05_pct: 8.3\n752\t    lz_stage1_vs_cn_stage3_correlation: 0.18\n753\t    stage1_emri_flagged_models: 3\n754\t    cn_flagged_same_models: 1\n755\t  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, θ, b, and d facets fit well because MFRM structure\n756\t    is appropriate for multi-judge data (these are just standard psychometric facets).\n757\t    But c_n shows substantial misfit: 36.4% of models have outfit > 1.3, indicating\n758\t    the rank-1 contamination model (c_n·e_i) doesn''t describe the residual patterns.\n759\t    This is because when true contamination is near-zero, the c_n parameters are fitting\n760\t    noise rather than signal — any nonzero c_n estimate absorbs heterogeneous residual\n761\t    variance, producing outfit. The lz (person-fit) from Stage 1 correlates only weakly\n762\t    (r=0.18) with c_n from Stage 3, meaning the two stages are identifying different\n763\t    ''aberrant'' patterns — further evidence that c_n is not measuring a coherent\n764\t    construct on this data. Divergent transitions (2.2%) cluster around c_n parameters\n765\t    near the half-Cauchy boundary. The model fits well OVERALL (global posterior predictive\n766\t    p=0.41) because c_n terms are near-zero and the model is effectively MFRM.'\n767\t  tables:\n768\t  - name: Facet Fit Summary\n769\t    columns:\n770\t    - Facet\n771\t    - N\n772\t    - Infit_mean\n773\t    - Outfit_mean\n774\t    - Misfit_pct\n775\t    - Interpretation\n776\t    rows:\n777\t    - - theta (ability)\n778\t      - 22\n779\t      - 1.02\n780\t      - 1.05\n781\t      - 4.5\n782\t      - Good fit\n783\t    - - b (difficulty)\n784\t      - 580\n785\t      - 0.99\n786\t      - 1.03\n787\t      - 6.2\n788\t      - Good fit\n789\t    - - d (severity)\n790\t      - 8\n791\t      - 1.01\n792\t      - 1.04\n793\t      - 12.5\n794\t      - Acceptable (1/8 judges borderline)\n795\t    - - c_n (contamination)\n796\t      - 22\n797\t      - 1.15\n798\t      - 1.28\n799\t      - 36.4\n800\t      - Poor fit — construct at floor\n801\t  - name: Convergence Diagnostics Detail\n802\t    columns:\n803\t    - Parameter_group\n804\t    - R_hat_median\n805\t    - R_hat_max\n806\t    - ESS_median\n807\t    - ESS_min\n808\t    - Divergent_pct\n809\t    rows:\n810\t    - - theta\n811\t      - 1.01\n812\t      - 1.03\n813\t      - 892\n814\t      - 412\n815\t      - 0.1\n816\t    - - b\n817\t      - 1.0\n818\t      - 1.02\n819\t      - 1105\n820\t      - 523\n821\t      - 0.2\n822\t    - - d\n823\t      - 1.01\n824\t      - 1.02\n825\t      - 945\n826\t      - 678\n827\t      - 0.1\n828\t    - - c_n\n829\t      - 1.05\n830\t      - 1.18\n831\t      - 156\n832\t      - 34\n833\t      - 1.8\n834\t  figures_description:\n835\t  - 'Wright map (person-item) from Stage 1: well-calibrated, items span ability range\n836\t    adequately'\n837\t  - 'c_n outfit vs c_n posterior mean: negative trend — models with higher c_n have\n838\t    WORSE fit, suggesting noise-fitting'\n839\t  - 'Trace plots for 3 worst-mixing c_n parameters: visible stuck chains, slow drift'\n840\t  analysis: 'Diagnostics reveal a split personality: G-MFRM as MFRM (θ,b,d) works\n841\t    well; the contamination extension (c_n) is poorly identified and misfitting. This\n842\t    is consistent with worst tier where c_n attempts to model a near-absent signal.\n843\t    The 36.4% c_n misfit rate would normally indicate model failure, but reframed:\n844\t    it shows the contamination term is unnecessary for this data. Pivot value: these\n845\t    diagnostics themselves demonstrate G-MFRM''s utility as a diagnostic framework\n846\t    — the misfit detection tells practitioners ''contamination is not a concern on\n847\t    this benchmark,'' which is actionable information.'\n848\t- name: confounding_formal_observation\n849\t  plan_id: exp-009\n850\t  description: Formal OVB bias derivation + Monte Carlo validation across realistic\n851\t    parameter ranges; synthetic c_n recovery\n852\t  expected_results:\n853\t    bias_formula_derived: true\n854\t    monte_carlo_n_conditions: 48\n855\t    monte_carlo_reps_per_condition: 100\n856\t    bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.15: 0.02\n857\t    bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.5: 0.08\n858\t    bias_magnitude_at_cn_1.5: 0.31\n859\t    bias_direction_prediction_accuracy: 0.94\n860\t    cn_recovery_rmse_at_real_magnitude: 0.42\n861\t    cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_real: 0.78\n862\t    cn_recovery_rmse_at_mvp_magnitude: 0.18\n863\t    cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_mvp: 0.91\n864\t    confounding_scatter_r_at_real_cn: 0.12\n865\t    confounding_scatter_r_at_mvp_cn: 0.65\n866\t    orthogonality_test_real_data: 0.09\n867\t  result_rationale: 'The formal OVB observation is valid regardless of contamination\n868\t    magnitude — it''s a mathematical identity. Monte Carlo confirms: bias direction\n869\t    prediction is 94% accurate across all conditions. BUT the practical bias magnitude\n870\t    scales linearly with c_n. At real-data contamination levels (c_n≈0.15), the sequential\n871\t    estimator bias is only 0.02 logits — negligible relative to posterior uncertainty.\n872\t    The confounding scatter r drops from 0.65 (mvp_002/003 regime) to 0.12 (real-data\n873\t    regime) because d_j and c_n·e_i are approximately orthogonal when c_n is near-zero.\n874\t    Synthetic c_n recovery shows the method WORKS at high contamination (RMSE=0.18,\n875\t    coverage=0.91 at mvp levels) but FAILS at real-data levels (RMSE=0.42, coverage=0.78)\n876\t    — the posterior is too wide to be informative. This confirms the core assumption:\n877\t    ''d_j and c_n·e_i approximately orthogonal in this dataset'' — confounding exists\n878\t    in theory but not in practice.'\n879\t  tables:\n880\t  - name: Monte Carlo Bias Magnitude by c_n Level\n881\t    columns:\n882\t    - c_n_regime\n883\t    - Bias_theta_RMSE\n884\t    - Scatter_r\n885\t    - Sequential_degradation_pct\n886\t    - Practical_significance\n887\t    rows:\n888\t    - - c_n = 0.15 (real)\n889\t      - 0.02\n890\t      - 0.12\n891\t      - 1.8\n892\t      - Negligible\n893\t    - - c_n = 0.50 (mvp_001)\n894\t      - 0.08\n895\t      - 0.38\n896\t      - 12.5\n897\t      - Marginal\n898\t    - - c_n = 1.00\n899\t      - 0.19\n900\t      - 0.52\n901\t      - 28.3\n902\t      - Moderate\n903\t    - - c_n = 1.50 (mvp_002/003)\n904\t      - 0.31\n905\t      - 0.65\n906\t      - 43.8\n907\t      - Large\n908\t  - name: Synthetic c_n Recovery at Real-Data Magnitude\n909\t    columns:\n910\t    - Metric\n911\t    - c_n=0.15\n912\t    - c_n=0.50\n913\t    - c_n=1.50\n914\t    rows:\n915\t    - - c_n RMSE\n916\t      - 0.42\n917\t      - 0.28\n918\t      - 0.18\n919\t    - - 95% CI coverage\n920\t      - 0.78\n921\t      - 0.87\n922\t      - 0.91\n923\t    - - c_n correlation (true vs fitted)\n924\t      - 0.35\n925\t      - 0.72\n926\t      - 0.92\n927\t    - - Pct models correctly ranked by c_n\n928\t      - 0.55\n929\t      - 0.78\n930\t      - 0.94\n931\t  figures_description:\n932\t  - 'Bias magnitude surface plot: x=c_n level, y=rho(d,c·e), z=theta bias — steep\n933\t    only in high-c_n, high-rho corner'\n934\t  - 'Confounding scatter at 4 c_n levels: panel plot showing scatter tightening to\n935\t    diagonal as c_n decreases'\n936\t  analysis: 'The formal observation remains a valid theoretical contribution — OVB\n937\t    exists regardless of magnitude. But the practical punchline under worst tier:\n938\t    the bias is negligible (0.02 logits) because contamination is too low to create\n939\t    meaningful confounding. The synthetic c_n recovery at real-data magnitude (correlation\n940\t    0.35) confirms G-MFRM cannot reliably rank models by contamination susceptibility\n941\t    when the signal is this weak. Pivot framing: ''we derive the confounding mechanism\n942\t    and show it activates under conditions X; current benchmarks happen to NOT meet\n943\t    these conditions; the correction is available when needed.'''\n944\t- name: wildbench_replication\n945\t  plan_id: exp-010\n946\t  description: Replicate G-MFRM pipeline on WildBench as third benchmark for cross-benchmark\n947\t    generalization\n948\t  expected_results:\n949\t    wildbench_n_items: 1024\n950\t    wildbench_n_judges: 6\n951\t    wildbench_kendall_tau_gmfrm: 0.74\n952\t    wildbench_kendall_tau_mfrm: 0.75\n953\t    wildbench_kendall_tau_uda: 0.76\n954\t    wildbench_delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_best: -0.02\n955\t    wildbench_cn_median: 0.09\n956\t    wildbench_cn_max: 0.38\n957\t    wildbench_ei_median: 0.08\n958\t    wildbench_convergence_rhat_max: 1.14\n959\t    wildbench_convergence_ess_min: 41\n960\t    cross_benchmark_cn_correlation: 0.28\n961\t    cross_benchmark_theta_correlation: 0.92\n962\t  result_rationale: WildBench uses more diverse, harder items sourced from real user\n963\t    interactions — these items are LESS likely to be contaminated than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard\n964\t    because they're more recent and less standardized. Under worst tier, WildBench\n965\t    shows even lower contamination (median c_n=0.09) than the other benchmarks. G-MFRM\n966\t    slightly underperforms both MFRM-only and UDA because the contamination parameters\n967\t    add noise. Cross-benchmark c_n correlation is weak (r=0.28) — models don't show\n968\t    consistent contamination patterns across benchmarks, suggesting what little contamination\n969\t    exists is benchmark-specific (item-level memorization) rather than model-level\n970\t    training data overlap. Cross-benchmark θ correlation (0.92) confirms ability estimates\n971\t    are stable regardless of contamination correction method.\n972\t  tables:\n973\t  - name: Cross-Benchmark Comparison\n974\t    columns:\n975\t    - Benchmark\n976\t    - tau_GMFRM\n977\t    - tau_MFRM\n978\t    - tau_UDA\n979\t    - cn_median\n980\t    - ei_median\n981\t    rows:\n982\t    - - MT-Bench\n983\t      - 0.76\n984\t      - 0.75\n985\t      - 0.78\n986\t      - 0.17\n987\t      - 0.14\n988\t    - - Arena-Hard\n989\t      - 0.78\n990\t      - 0.77\n991\t      - 0.79\n992\t      - 0.14\n993\t      - 0.11\n994\t    - - WildBench\n995\t      - 0.74\n996\t      - 0.75\n997\t      - 0.76\n998\t      - 0.09\n999\t      - 0.08\n1000\t  figures_description:\n1001\t  - 'Cross-benchmark c_n scatter matrix: 3 panels showing weak correlations between\n1002\t    benchmarks — contamination is benchmark-specific, not model-intrinsic'\n1003\t  analysis: 'WildBench replication confirms worst-tier pattern generalizes: negligible\n1004\t    contamination, no ranking benefit from G-MFRM. The low cross-benchmark c_n correlation\n1005\t    (0.28) suggests that contamination — what little exists — is item-specific rather\n1006\t    than reflecting stable model-level susceptibility. This weakens the c_n construct\n1007\t    validity further but supports the ''benchmarks are clean'' narrative.'\n1008\t- name: adversarial_robustness\n1009\t  plan_id: exp-011\n1010\t  description: Test G-MFRM robustness against adversarial contamination following\n1011\t    Wang et al. (ICLR 2026)\n1012\t  expected_results:\n1013\t    adversarial_injection_levels:\n1014\t    - 0.0\n1015\t    - 0.3\n1016\t    - 0.5\n1017\t    - 1.0\n1018\t    - 1.5\n1019\t    tau_gmfrm_at_0.0: 0.77\n1020\t    tau_gmfrm_at_0.3: 0.76\n1021\t    tau_gmfrm_at_0.5: 0.78\n1022\t    tau_gmfrm_at_1.0: 0.82\n1023\t    tau_gmfrm_at_1.5: 0.87\n1024\t    tau_sequential_at_1.5: 0.79\n1025\t    tau_mfrm_at_1.5: 0.75\n1026\t    crossover_point_cn: 0.7\n1027\t    cn_recovery_at_injection_1.5: 0.81\n1028\t    detection_power_at_real_cn: 0.15\n1029\t    detection_power_at_injected_1.5: 0.92\n1030\t  result_rationale: Under worst tier, natural contamination is negligible — but we\n1031\t    can INJECT synthetic contamination to demonstrate G-MFRM works when contamination\n1032\t    IS present. By artificially inflating scores for specific models on high-e_i items,\n1033\t    we create a controlled setting. At injection level 1.5 (matching mvp_002/003),\n1034\t    G-MFRM recovers well (τ=0.87 vs sequential's 0.79). The crossover point where\n1035\t    G-MFRM starts outperforming is c_n≈0.7 — above real-data levels but demonstrating\n1036\t    the method's potential. Detection power at real c_n levels is only 0.15 (cannot\n1037\t    detect what isn't there), but jumps to 0.92 at injected 1.5. This experiment redeems\n1038\t    the method theoretically while confirming it's unnecessary on current benchmarks.\n1039\t  tables:\n1040\t  - name: Adversarial Injection Results\n1041\t    columns:\n1042\t    - Injection_level\n1043\t    - tau_GMFRM\n1044\t    - tau_Sequential\n1045\t    - tau_MFRM\n1046\t    - cn_recovery_corr\n1047\t    - Detection_power\n1048\t    rows:\n1049\t    - - 0.0\n1050\t      - 0.77\n1051\t      - 0.76\n1052\t      - 0.77\n1053\t      - 0.35\n1054\t      - 0.15\n1055\t    - - 0.3\n1056\t      - 0.76\n1057\t      - 0.76\n1058\t      - 0.76\n1059\t      - 0.42\n1060\t      - 0.25\n1061\t    - - 0.5\n1062\t      - 0.78\n1063\t      - 0.77\n1064\t      - 0.76\n1065\t      - 0.58\n1066\t      - 0.45\n1067\t    - - 1.0\n1068\t      - 0.82\n1069\t      - 0.79\n1070\t      - 0.75\n1071\t      - 0.74\n1072\t      - 0.78\n1073\t    - - 1.5\n1074\t      - 0.87\n1075\t      - 0.79\n1076\t      - 0.75\n1077\t      - 0.81\n1078\t      - 0.92\n1079\t  figures_description:\n1080\t  - 'Crossover plot: G-MFRM vs baselines as function of injected contamination level\n1081\t    — lines cross around c_n=0.7, with G-MFRM pulling ahead in high-contamination\n1082\t    regime'\n1083\t  - Annotated vertical line at 'real data level' (c_n≈0.15) showing all methods equivalent\n1084\t    there\n1085\t  analysis: 'Adversarial robustness provides the strongest pivot evidence: G-MFRM\n1086\t    works when contamination exists, it just doesn''t exist on current benchmarks.\n1087\t    The crossover at c_n=0.7 gives a principled threshold for when G-MFRM becomes\n1088\t    necessary. Framing: ''G-MFRM is an insurance policy — unnecessary today but protective\n1089\t    against future contamination increases as training data grows.'' Also validates\n1090\t    the methodology for historical benchmarks (e.g., MMLU original) where contamination\n1091\t    IS known to be high.'\n1092\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e3840f6f-5cda-4115-aa82-47bd4eaaa707","uuid":"3fd49433-17c1-4d3f-a900-231cfc70ebec","timestamp":"2026-05-22T04:59:38.522Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-prod43/projects/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260522T041217/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Real contamination is negligible on current benchmarks — G-MFRM\n  cannot improve rankings because there is nothing substantial to correct. Pivot to:\n  (1) G-MFRM as principled diagnostic audit framework, (2) the null finding itself\n  as contribution challenging contamination assumptions, (3) identifiability/methodology\n  contribution independent of effect size. Target ARR Findings or AAAI evaluation\n  workshop.'\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: multi_judge_data_collection\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: Collect multi-judge evaluation data on MT-Bench and Arena-Hard with\n    8 judges × 20+ target models\n  expected_results:\n    total_judges_collected: 8\n    total_target_models: 22\n    total_judgments_mtbench: 14080\n    total_judgments_arenahard: 35200\n    position_swap_agreement: 0.72\n    inter_judge_agreement_fleiss_kappa: 0.38\n    judge_severity_range_raw: 0.85\n    missing_data_pct: 3.2\n    api_cost_usd: 870\n    gpu_hours_used: 118\n  result_rationale: Data collection itself is scenario-independent — the judges produce\n    scores regardless of whether contamination exists. However, under worst tier the\n    inter-judge agreement is moderate (κ≈0.38) because judges disagree primarily on\n    difficulty/style rather than contamination-driven inflation. Position swap agreement\n    is typical (0.72) indicating normal positional bias. The raw judge severity range\n    (0.85 logits) is present but moderate — some judges (e.g., Qwen3.6-27B) show compressed\n    scoring ranges (<0.3 logit range), limiting MFRM's value for those facets.\n  tables:\n  - name: Judge Collection Summary\n    columns:\n    - Judge\n    - N_judgments\n    - Mean_score\n    - SD\n    - Severity_range\n    - Position_bias\n    rows:\n    - - GPT-5.5\n      - 4400\n      - 7.82\n      - 1.21\n      - ref\n      - 0.08\n    - - Claude Opus 4.7\n      - 4400\n      - 7.45\n      - 1.35\n      - -0.37\n      - 0.12\n    - - Claude Sonnet 4.6\n      - 4400\n      - 7.61\n      - 1.18\n      - -0.21\n      - 0.09\n    - - DeepSeek-V4-Pro\n      - 4400\n      - 7.28\n      - 1.42\n      - -0.54\n      - 0.15\n    - - Qwen3-235B-A22B\n      - 4400\n      - 7.53\n      - 1.08\n      - -0.29\n      - 0.11\n    - - Qwen3.6-27B\n      - 4400\n      - 7.91\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.09\n      - 0.06\n    - - Llama 4 Maverick\n      - 4400\n      - 7.15\n      - 1.48\n      - -0.67\n      - 0.14\n    - - Gemini 2.5 Pro\n      - 4400\n      - 7.68\n      - 1.25\n      - -0.14\n      - 0.1\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Score distribution heatmap (judges × models): relatively uniform inflation pattern\n    across models — no clear contamination-driven ''spikes'' for specific model-item\n    pairs'\n  analysis: 'Data quality is adequate for MFRM fitting but the score distributions\n    already hint at worst-tier outcome: no judge shows dramatically different severity\n    for known-contaminated vs clean models. The compressed scoring range of Qwen3.6-27B\n    (SD=0.82) will limit its contribution to facet separation.'\n- name: item_exposure_estimation\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: Estimate per-item exposure e_i via three-source pipeline (performance\n    gap, temporal, LogProber)\n  expected_results:\n    ei_mean: 0.18\n    ei_median: 0.12\n    ei_std: 0.22\n    ei_max: 0.71\n    ei_iqr:\n    - 0.04\n    - 0.28\n    pct_items_ei_above_0.5: 8.5\n    performance_gap_signal_snr: 1.2\n    temporal_signal_coverage_pct: 35\n    logprober_coverage_pct: 45\n    source_intercorrelation_gap_temporal: 0.28\n    source_intercorrelation_gap_logprober: 0.31\n    source_intercorrelation_temporal_logprober: 0.19\n    weighted_composite_internal_consistency_alpha: 0.24\n    spline_correction_r2: 0.42\n    reference_variant_quality_check_pass_pct: 78\n  result_rationale: Under worst tier, e_i pipeline produces weak/noisy signal (effective\n    σ_e ≈ 0.3–0.5 in the core assumption). Most items show near-zero exposure because\n    current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2) were designed to resist\n    memorization. The three sources have low intercorrelation (0.19–0.31) indicating\n    they capture different noise rather than converging on a true signal. Internal\n    consistency α=0.24 is far below psychometric acceptability (0.70). The few items\n    with e_i > 0.5 are likely legacy items from MT-Bench v1 that survived into the\n    refresh. LogProber coverage is limited (45%) because many target models lack logprob\n    APIs. The spline difficulty correction only explains 42% of variance in raw performance\n    gaps — most of the gap is difficulty-driven, not contamination-driven.\n  tables:\n  - name: Exposure Source Contribution\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - Coverage_pct\n    - Mean_signal\n    - SD\n    - SNR\n    - Correlation_with_composite\n    rows:\n    - - Performance gap (primary)\n      - 100\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.19\n      - 1.2\n      - 0.78\n    - - Temporal signal\n      - 35\n      - 0.21\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.8\n      - 0.52\n    - - LogProber\n      - 45\n      - 0.14\n      - 0.24\n      - 0.9\n      - 0.61\n  - name: e_i Distribution by Benchmark\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - N_items\n    - Median_ei\n    - Mean_ei\n    - Pct_above_0.5\n    - Max_ei\n    rows:\n    - - MT-Bench\n      - 80\n      - 0.14\n      - 0.2\n      - 10.0\n      - 0.71\n    - - Arena-Hard\n      - 500\n      - 0.11\n      - 0.17\n      - 7.8\n      - 0.63\n  figures_description:\n  - 'e_i histogram: heavily right-skewed with mode near 0, long thin tail — most items\n    effectively unexposed'\n  - 'Source agreement scatter: three panels showing weak positive correlations between\n    each pair of sources, large scatter'\n  analysis: The e_i pipeline's internal consistency (α=0.24) is a critical weakness.\n    The signal is real but noisy — identifiable in aggregate but unreliable at per-item\n    level. This means Stage 3 fitting will have limited power to separate c_n from\n    noise in e_i. The low median (0.12) confirms that most benchmark items are effectively\n    clean, leaving little contamination signal for G-MFRM to exploit.\n- name: gmfrm_real_data_fit\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: Fit G-MFRM three-stage pipeline on real data; compare fair scores with\n    Chatbot Arena (Kendall-τ)\n  expected_results:\n    stage1_mfrm_convergence_rhat_max: 1.02\n    stage1_mfrm_ess_min: 312\n    stage3_joint_convergence_rhat_max: 1.12\n    stage3_joint_ess_min: 58\n    stage3_cn_rhat_max: 1.18\n    stage3_cn_ess_min: 34\n    kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_mtbench: 0.76\n    kendall_tau_arena_gmfrm_arenahard: 0.78\n    kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_mtbench: 0.75\n    kendall_tau_arena_mfrm_only_arenahard: 0.77\n    cn_median_fitted: 0.15\n    cn_mean_fitted: 0.22\n    cn_max_fitted: 0.61\n    cn_95ci_width_median: 0.48\n    theta_fair_vs_theta_mfrm_rank_correlation: 0.97\n    pct_models_rank_change_geq_2: 9.1\n    divergent_transitions_pct: 2.8\n    runtime_hours: 8.5\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, real c_n distribution has median < 0.3 (fitted\n    median=0.15). Most models have near-zero contamination susceptibility — the c_n\n    posterior piles up near the half-Cauchy prior''s mode. The few models with c_n\n    > 0.4 have wide credible intervals (median CI width=0.48) because e_i signal is\n    weak. Stage 3 convergence is problematic: R̂=1.12 overall and up to 1.18 for c_n\n    parameters specifically, with ESS as low as 34 for some c_n. This is because the\n    data provides minimal information about c_n when e_i is near-zero for most items\n    — the likelihood surface is nearly flat in the c_n direction. The resulting fair\n    scores θ̂ barely differ from MFRM-only θ̂ (rank correlation 0.97), meaning the\n    contamination correction has negligible practical effect. Kendall-τ with Arena\n    is 0.76–0.78, within the expected range for this tier but not meaningfully better\n    than MFRM-only.'\n  tables:\n  - name: G-MFRM Fit Summary by Benchmark\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - R_hat_max\n    - ESS_min\n    - Divergent_pct\n    - cn_median\n    - cn_max\n    - tau_Arena\n    rows:\n    - - MT-Bench\n      - 1.12\n      - 58\n      - 2.8\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.76\n    - - Arena-Hard\n      - 1.08\n      - 72\n      - 1.5\n      - 0.14\n      - 0.53\n      - 0.78\n  - name: Fitted c_n Distribution (Top 5 Models)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - c_n_mean\n    - c_n_95CI_low\n    - c_n_95CI_high\n    - CI_width\n    - R_hat\n    rows:\n    - - Model_A (suspected legacy)\n      - 0.61\n      - 0.18\n      - 1.12\n      - 0.94\n      - 1.15\n    - - Model_B\n      - 0.43\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.89\n      - 0.81\n      - 1.18\n    - - Model_C\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.05\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.69\n      - 1.11\n    - - Model_D\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.01\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.57\n      - 1.09\n    - - Model_E\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.01\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.51\n      - 1.07\n  figures_description:\n  - 'c_n posterior density plot: most posteriors overlap heavily with zero; only 2-3\n    models have posterior mass clearly above 0.3'\n  - 'θ_fair vs θ_mfrm scatter: nearly perfect diagonal, with 2-3 outliers shifted\n    slightly downward (the high-c_n models)'\n  - 'Trace plots for c_n parameters: visible autocorrelation, slow mixing for models\n    with c_n near boundary'\n  analysis: 'Stage 1 MFRM fits well (R̂=1.02) — judge severity and item difficulty\n    are well-identified. But Stage 3 struggles with c_n: the combination of weak e_i\n    signal and low true contamination means the data cannot distinguish c_n=0 from\n    c_n=0.3 for most models. The 2.8% divergent transitions on MT-Bench indicate the\n    posterior geometry is problematic near the c_n≈0 boundary. Practically, fair scores\n    θ̂ are indistinguishable from MFRM-only estimates for 20/22 models. The G-MFRM\n    ''correction'' affects only 2-3 models with possible legacy contamination, and\n    even those corrections are within posterior uncertainty.'\n- name: baseline_comparison\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: Compare G-MFRM against 8 baselines; Kendall-τ and Spearman-ρ with Chatbot\n    Arena; rank change analysis\n  expected_results:\n    kendall_tau_raw_average: 0.74\n    kendall_tau_mfrm_only: 0.77\n    kendall_tau_sequential: 0.76\n    kendall_tau_cyclicjudge: 0.78\n    kendall_tau_cyclicjudge_discard: 0.77\n    kendall_tau_dcr: 0.75\n    kendall_tau_uda: 0.79\n    kendall_tau_prefleak_calib: 0.76\n    kendall_tau_gmfrm: 0.77\n    best_baseline: UDA\n    delta_tau_vs_best_baseline: -0.02\n    delta_tau_vs_mfrm_only: 0.0\n    wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_uda: 0.34\n    wilcoxon_p_gmfrm_vs_cyclicjudge: 0.62\n    spearman_rho_gmfrm: 0.89\n    spearman_rho_best_baseline: 0.91\n    rank_change_models_geq2_positions: 2\n    top5_stability_gmfrm: 0.8\n    top5_stability_best_baseline: 0.8\n    post_hoc_power_for_observed_delta: 0.12\n    n_models: 22\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, Δτ is in [-0.02, 0.01] range. G-MFRM (τ=0.77)\n    is actually slightly BELOW UDA (τ=0.79) — UDA''s neural K-factor adjustment captures\n    judge-specific biases that are the dominant source of ranking noise when contamination\n    is negligible. CyclicJudge (τ=0.78) also edges out G-MFRM because round-robin\n    bias elimination is effective when contamination is near-zero. The Wilcoxon test\n    is massively underpowered (power=0.12 at N=22 models for detecting Δτ=0.02) —\n    we cannot distinguish methods statistically. G-MFRM ties MFRM-only (both τ=0.77),\n    confirming the contamination correction adds nothing when there''s nothing to\n    correct. Rank changes are minimal: only 2 models shift ≥2 positions between G-MFRM\n    and best baseline, and these are within posterior uncertainty. This is the fatal\n    result for Claim 3.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Main Comparison Table\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Kendall_tau\n    - Spearman_rho\n    - Rank_changes_geq2\n    - Top5_stable\n    rows:\n    - - Raw multi-judge average\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.86\n      - ref\n      - ref\n    - - MFRM-only (Stage 1)\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.89\n      - 3\n      - 4/5\n    - - Sequential (MFRM→contamination)\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.88\n      - 2\n      - 4/5\n    - - CyclicJudge\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.9\n      - 3\n      - 4/5\n    - - CyclicJudge + item discard\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.89\n      - 2\n      - 4/5\n    - - DCR Factor\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.87\n      - 2\n      - 4/5\n    - - UDA\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.91\n      - 4\n      - 4/5\n    - - Preference Leakage calibration\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.88\n      - 2\n      - 4/5\n    - - G-MFRM (ours)\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.89\n      - 2\n      - 4/5\n  - name: Statistical Tests\n    columns:\n    - Comparison\n    - Delta_tau\n    - Wilcoxon_p\n    - Power_at_alpha_0.05\n    - Significant?\n    rows:\n    - - G-MFRM vs UDA\n      - -0.02\n      - 0.34\n      - 0.12\n      - 'No'\n    - - G-MFRM vs CyclicJudge\n      - -0.01\n      - 0.62\n      - 0.08\n      - 'No'\n    - - G-MFRM vs MFRM-only\n      - 0.0\n      - 0.91\n      - 0.05\n      - 'No'\n    - - G-MFRM vs Sequential\n      - 0.01\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.09\n      - 'No'\n    - - G-MFRM vs Raw average\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.22\n      - 'No'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bootstrap confidence interval plot: all methods'' τ CIs overlap heavily; G-MFRM\n    CI centered at 0.77 with [0.71, 0.83] — indistinguishable from 5 other methods'\n  - 'Rank change alluvial diagram: nearly static rankings across methods; 2-3 models\n    shift 1-2 positions, driven by judge bias correction not contamination'\n  analysis: 'Claim 3 is dead. G-MFRM does not improve over best baselines — it slightly\n    underperforms UDA. Post-hoc power analysis (power=0.12) confirms the study is\n    fundamentally underpowered to detect the observed effect size with N=22 models.\n    Even with 50 models, power would be ~0.25 for Δτ=0.02. The ranking improvement\n    is non-significant by any standard. MFRM-only and G-MFRM produce identical τ,\n    confirming the contamination gating mechanism adds no signal. Pivot narrative:\n    G-MFRM ''degrades gracefully'' to MFRM level when contamination is absent — this\n    is a feature, not a failure.'\n- name: contamination_magnitude_calibration\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: Estimate real contamination magnitude; determine if c_n matches mvp_001\n    (weak) or mvp_002 (strong)\n  expected_results:\n    real_cn_median: 0.15\n    real_cn_mean: 0.22\n    real_cn_max: 0.61\n    real_cn_iqr:\n    - 0.04\n    - 0.32\n    pct_models_cn_above_0.5: 9.1\n    pct_models_cn_above_1.0: 0.0\n    closest_mvp_match: below mvp_001\n    mvp_001_cn_mean_was: 0.5\n    ratio_real_to_mvp001: 0.44\n    constat_delta_correlation_with_cn: 0.32\n    constat_delta_median: 0.08\n    cn_distribution_shape: heavily right-skewed, mode near 0\n    bimodality_coefficient: 0.31\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, real c_n median is in [0.05, 0.3] — we get\n    0.15, which is BELOW even mvp_001''s assumption of c~0.5. The distribution is\n    not bimodal but heavily right-skewed with mode near zero: most models have negligible\n    contamination, 2-3 have moderate (0.4-0.6), none reach 1.0. This is well below\n    the regime where G-MFRM''s gating mechanism has meaningful leverage. The ConStat\n    per-model delta validates only weakly (r=0.32) because both signals are near-floor\n    — correlation is attenuated by floor effects and measurement noise. The finding\n    itself IS informative: current benchmarks (MT-Bench 2024 refresh, Arena-Hard v2)\n    appear to have effectively low contamination. This challenges assumptions in contamination\n    literature that training data overlap produces large performance inflation on\n    these specific benchmarks.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Contamination Magnitude Comparison\n    columns:\n    - Source\n    - cn_median\n    - cn_mean\n    - cn_max\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - MVP synthetic (mvp_001)\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.5\n      - 1.0\n      - Assumed moderate\n    - - MVP synthetic (mvp_002/003)\n      - 1.5\n      - 1.5\n      - 2.5\n      - Assumed strong\n    - - Real data (this experiment)\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.61\n      - Negligible to weak\n  - name: Per-Model c_n Estimates\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - c_n_posterior_mean\n    - 95CI\n    - ConStat_delta\n    - Category\n    rows:\n    - - GPT-5.5\n      - 0.08\n      - '[0.01, 0.29]'\n      - 0.05\n      - negligible\n    - - Claude Opus 4.7\n      - 0.05\n      - '[0.00, 0.21]'\n      - 0.03\n      - negligible\n    - - Llama 4 405B\n      - 0.35\n      - '[0.06, 0.72]'\n      - 0.15\n      - weak\n    - - Qwen3-235B\n      - 0.43\n      - '[0.08, 0.89]'\n      - 0.18\n      - weak-moderate\n    - - DeepSeek-V4\n      - 0.61\n      - '[0.18, 1.12]'\n      - 0.22\n      - moderate (max)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'c_n distribution: kernel density showing heavy pile-up near 0, thin tail to 0.6;\n    overlaid with mvp_001 and mvp_002 assumed distributions for visual comparison\n    of how much weaker real signal is'\n  - 'ConStat delta vs G-MFRM c_n scatter: weak positive trend (r=0.32), large scatter,\n    most points clustered near origin'\n  analysis: 'This is the definitive worst-tier signal. Real contamination is far below\n    synthetic assumptions. The mvp_002/003 success (43.8% RMSE reduction) required\n    c_n~1.5 — real data has c_n~0.15 (10× weaker). Even mvp_001 assumed c~0.5. The\n    G-MFRM''s gating mechanism is solving a problem that barely exists on these benchmarks.\n    Pivot implications: (1) the null finding about contamination magnitude is itself\n    publishable — benchmarks may be cleaner than assumed; (2) G-MFRM''s value is as\n    a diagnostic tool that CONFIRMS low contamination rather than correcting for high\n    contamination; (3) need to find or construct a benchmark with known high contamination\n    for validation case.'\n- name: judge_count_sensitivity\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: Vary judges (2,4,6,8); measure G-MFRM degradation; leave-one-judge-out\n    residual diagnostic\n  expected_results:\n    tau_8_judges: 0.77\n    tau_6_judges: 0.76\n    tau_4_judges: 0.75\n    tau_2_judges: 0.73\n    tau_mfrm_only_8_judges: 0.77\n    tau_mfrm_only_4_judges: 0.75\n    delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_8j: 0.0\n    delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_6j: 0.0\n    delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_4j: 0.0\n    delta_gmfrm_vs_mfrm_2j: -0.01\n    cn_identifiability_threshold_judges: not reached\n    leave_one_out_max_residual_change: 0.04\n    leave_one_out_same_family_bias_detected: false\n    convergence_2j_rhat_max: 1.25\n    convergence_4j_rhat_max: 1.15\n    convergence_6j_rhat_max: 1.1\n  result_rationale: Under worst tier, G-MFRM never outperforms MFRM-only at any judge\n    count because contamination is negligible — the gating mechanism adds parameters\n    without adding signal. At 2 judges, G-MFRM actually slightly underperforms MFRM-only\n    (Δτ=-0.01) due to over-parameterization with insufficient data. Convergence degrades\n    progressively as judges decrease (R̂=1.25 at 2 judges) because c_n parameters\n    are completely unidentifiable with sparse judge coverage and weak e_i. The leave-one-judge-out\n    diagnostic shows no systematic same-family bias pattern — residual changes are\n    small (max 0.04) because contamination effects are too weak to create detectable\n    judge-model interactions. The practical conclusion is that with ≥4 judges, G-MFRM\n    is harmless (degrades gracefully to MFRM level) but never beneficial for ranking.\n  tables:\n  - name: Judge Count Sensitivity\n    columns:\n    - N_judges\n    - tau_GMFRM\n    - tau_MFRM\n    - Delta\n    - R_hat_max\n    - ESS_min_cn\n    rows:\n    - - 8\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.0\n      - 1.12\n      - 58\n    - - 6\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.0\n      - 1.1\n      - 65\n    - - 4\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.0\n      - 1.15\n      - 42\n    - - 2\n      - 0.73\n      - 0.74\n      - -0.01\n      - 1.25\n      - 22\n  - name: Leave-One-Judge-Out Residual Diagnostic\n    columns:\n    - Removed_judge\n    - Family_overlap?\n    - Max_residual_change\n    - Systematic_bias?\n    rows:\n    - - GPT-5.5\n      - Yes (GPT family targets)\n      - 0.04\n      - 'No'\n    - - Claude Opus 4.7\n      - Yes (Claude targets)\n      - 0.03\n      - 'No'\n    - - DeepSeek-V4-Pro\n      - Yes (DeepSeek targets)\n      - 0.02\n      - 'No'\n    - - Qwen3-235B-A22B\n      - Yes (Qwen targets)\n      - 0.03\n      - 'No'\n    - - Llama 4 Maverick\n      - Yes (Llama targets)\n      - 0.02\n      - 'No'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Performance degradation curve: two nearly overlapping lines (G-MFRM and MFRM-only)\n    both declining gently from 8→2 judges; gap is zero or negative throughout'\n  - 'R̂ vs judge count: exponential increase below 4 judges for c_n parameters specifically,\n    while θ and d parameters remain well-behaved'\n  analysis: 'This experiment confirms G-MFRM adds nothing over MFRM-only at any judge\n    count in the worst tier. The contamination facet is unidentifiable in practice.\n    However, the graceful degradation finding is publishable as a safety property:\n    G-MFRM doesn''t HARM rankings when contamination is absent. The leave-one-judge-out\n    diagnostic is useful as a methodological contribution regardless of contamination\n    magnitude — it provides a principled way to detect preference leakage without\n    requiring contamination to be present.'\n- name: exposure_indicator_ablation\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'Ablation: contribution of each exposure source to e_i quality; leave-one-out\n    analysis'\n  expected_results:\n    full_pipeline_alpha: 0.24\n    leave_out_performance_gap_alpha: 0.15\n    leave_out_temporal_alpha: 0.22\n    leave_out_logprober_alpha: 0.2\n    performance_gap_unique_contribution: 0.09\n    temporal_unique_contribution: 0.02\n    logprober_unique_contribution: 0.04\n    performance_gap_only_tau: 0.76\n    temporal_only_tau: 0.75\n    logprober_only_tau: 0.75\n    full_pipeline_tau: 0.77\n    no_ei_mfrm_only_tau: 0.77\n    delta_any_source_vs_no_ei:\n    - -0.02\n    - 0.01\n  result_rationale: Under worst tier, e_i pipeline has internal consistency α=0.24\n    (floor). When we ablate sources, the full pipeline (α=0.24) is already so weak\n    that removing any single source barely changes downstream τ. The performance gap\n    is the strongest single source but still weak (unique contribution = 0.09 to α).\n    Temporal and LogProber add negligible incremental signal. Critically, ALL configurations\n    of e_i produce the same downstream τ (0.75–0.77) because the contamination correction\n    itself is inconsequential — varying e_i quality doesn't matter when c_n ≈ 0. The\n    'no e_i' condition (MFRM-only, τ=0.77) matches or beats all e_i conditions, confirming\n    the entire exposure estimation pipeline is effort without payoff on these benchmarks.\n  tables:\n  - name: Exposure Source Ablation\n    columns:\n    - Configuration\n    - Alpha\n    - e_i_mean\n    - e_i_SD\n    - Downstream_tau\n    - Delta_vs_MFRM_only\n    rows:\n    - - Full pipeline (3 sources)\n      - 0.24\n      - 0.18\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.0\n    - - Without performance gap\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.16\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.76\n      - -0.01\n    - - Without temporal\n      - 0.22\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.21\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.0\n    - - Without LogProber\n      - 0.2\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.23\n      - 0.76\n      - -0.01\n    - - Performance gap only\n      - N/A\n      - 0.15\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.76\n      - -0.01\n    - - No e_i (MFRM-only)\n      - N/A\n      - N/A\n      - N/A\n      - 0.77\n      - ref\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart of unique variance contribution per source: all bars near zero, performance\n    gap slightly taller but still below meaningful threshold'\n  - 'Downstream τ forest plot: all configurations'' CIs overlap completely, including\n    MFRM-only baseline'\n  analysis: 'The ablation confirms that no e_i source meaningfully contributes to\n    ranking quality under worst tier. This is not a pipeline failure per se — it''s\n    that the signal being measured (item exposure) is genuinely near-zero on these\n    benchmarks. Pivot narrative: the pipeline is VALIDATED for measuring exposure\n    (internal structure exists, α=0.24 > 0), but the underlying construct is at floor.\n    This supports the ''benchmarks are clean'' narrative contribution.'\n- name: facet_fit_diagnostics\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'Measurement quality: infit/outfit, person-fit, divergent transitions,\n    posterior predictive checks'\n  expected_results:\n    theta_infit_mean: 1.02\n    theta_outfit_mean: 1.05\n    theta_misfit_pct: 4.5\n    b_infit_mean: 0.99\n    b_outfit_mean: 1.03\n    b_misfit_pct: 6.2\n    d_infit_mean: 1.01\n    d_outfit_mean: 1.04\n    d_misfit_pct: 12.5\n    cn_infit_mean: 1.15\n    cn_outfit_mean: 1.28\n    cn_misfit_pct: 36.4\n    divergent_transitions_total: 142\n    divergent_transitions_pct: 2.2\n    posterior_predictive_p_global: 0.41\n    posterior_predictive_p_items_below_0.05_pct: 8.3\n    lz_stage1_vs_cn_stage3_correlation: 0.18\n    stage1_emri_flagged_models: 3\n    cn_flagged_same_models: 1\n  result_rationale: 'Under worst tier, θ, b, and d facets fit well because MFRM structure\n    is appropriate for multi-judge data (these are just standard psychometric facets).\n    But c_n shows substantial misfit: 36.4% of models have outfit > 1.3, indicating\n    the rank-1 contamination model (c_n·e_i) doesn''t describe the residual patterns.\n    This is because when true contamination is near-zero, the c_n parameters are fitting\n    noise rather than signal — any nonzero c_n estimate absorbs heterogeneous residual\n    variance, producing outfit. The lz (person-fit) from Stage 1 correlates only weakly\n    (r=0.18) with c_n from Stage 3, meaning the two stages are identifying different\n    ''aberrant'' patterns — further evidence that c_n is not measuring a coherent\n    construct on this data. Divergent transitions (2.2%) cluster around c_n parameters\n    near the half-Cauchy boundary. The model fits well OVERALL (global posterior predictive\n    p=0.41) because c_n terms are near-zero and the model is effectively MFRM.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Facet Fit Summary\n    columns:\n    - Facet\n    - N\n    - Infit_mean\n    - Outfit_mean\n    - Misfit_pct\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - theta (ability)\n      - 22\n      - 1.02\n      - 1.05\n      - 4.5\n      - Good fit\n    - - b (difficulty)\n      - 580\n      - 0.99\n      - 1.03\n      - 6.2\n      - Good fit\n    - - d (severity)\n      - 8\n      - 1.01\n      - 1.04\n      - 12.5\n      - Acceptable (1/8 judges borderline)\n    - - c_n (contamination)\n      - 22\n      - 1.15\n      - 1.28\n      - 36.4\n      - Poor fit — construct at floor\n  - name: Convergence Diagnostics Detail\n    columns:\n    - Parameter_group\n    - R_hat_median\n    - R_hat_max\n    - ESS_median\n    - ESS_min\n    - Divergent_pct\n    rows:\n    - - theta\n      - 1.01\n      - 1.03\n      - 892\n      - 412\n      - 0.1\n    - - b\n      - 1.0\n      - 1.02\n      - 1105\n      - 523\n      - 0.2\n    - - d\n      - 1.01\n      - 1.02\n      - 945\n      - 678\n      - 0.1\n    - - c_n\n      - 1.05\n      - 1.18\n      - 156\n      - 34\n      - 1.8\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Wright map (person-item) from Stage 1: well-calibrated, items span ability range\n    adequately'\n  - 'c_n outfit vs c_n posterior mean: negative trend — models with higher c_n have\n    WORSE fit, suggesting noise-fitting'\n  - 'Trace plots for 3 worst-mixing c_n parameters: visible stuck chains, slow drift'\n  analysis: 'Diagnostics reveal a split personality: G-MFRM as MFRM (θ,b,d) works\n    well; the contamination extension (c_n) is poorly identified and misfitting. This\n    is consistent with worst tier where c_n attempts to model a near-absent signal.\n    The 36.4% c_n misfit rate would normally indicate model failure, but reframed:\n    it shows the contamination term is unnecessary for this data. Pivot value: these\n    diagnostics themselves demonstrate G-MFRM''s utility as a diagnostic framework\n    — the misfit detection tells practitioners ''contamination is not a concern on\n    this benchmark,'' which is actionable information.'\n- name: confounding_formal_observation\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: Formal OVB bias derivation + Monte Carlo validation across realistic\n    parameter ranges; synthetic c_n recovery\n  expected_results:\n    bias_formula_derived: true\n    monte_carlo_n_conditions: 48\n    monte_carlo_reps_per_condition: 100\n    bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.15: 0.02\n    bias_magnitude_at_cn_0.5: 0.08\n    bias_magnitude_at_cn_1.5: 0.31\n    bias_direction_prediction_accuracy: 0.94\n    cn_recovery_rmse_at_real_magnitude: 0.42\n    cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_real: 0.78\n    cn_recovery_rmse_at_mvp_magnitude: 0.18\n    cn_recovery_95ci_coverage_at_mvp: 0.91\n    confounding_scatter_r_at_real_cn: 0.12\n    confounding_scatter_r_at_mvp_cn: 0.65\n    orthogonality_test_real_data: 0.09\n  result_rationale: 'The formal OVB observation is valid regardless of contamination\n    magnitude — it''s a mathematical identity. Monte Carlo confirms: bias direction\n    prediction is 94% accurate across all conditions. BUT the practical bias magnitude\n    scales linearly with c_n. At real-data contamination levels (c_n≈0.15), the sequential\n    estimator bias is only 0.02 logits — negligible relative to posterior uncertainty.\n    The confounding scatter r drops from 0.65 (mvp_002/003 regime) to 0.12 (real-data\n    regime) because d_j and c_n·e_i are approximately orthogonal when c_n is near-zero.\n    Synthetic c_n recovery shows the method WORKS at high contamination (RMSE=0.18,\n    coverage=0.91 at mvp levels) but FAILS at real-data levels (RMSE=0.42, coverage=0.78)\n    — the posterior is too wide to be informative. This confirms the core assumption:\n    ''d_j and c_n·e_i approximately orthogonal in this dataset'' — confounding exists\n    in theory but not in practice.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Monte Carlo Bias Magnitude by c_n Level\n    columns:\n    - c_n_regime\n    - Bias_theta_RMSE\n    - Scatter_r\n    - Sequential_degradation_pct\n    - Practical_significance\n    rows:\n    - - c_n = 0.15 (real)\n      - 0.02\n      - 0.12\n      - 1.8\n      - Negligible\n    - - c_n = 0.50 (mvp_001)\n      - 0.08\n      - 0.38\n      - 12.5\n      - Marginal\n    - - c_n = 1.00\n      - 0.19\n      - 0.52\n      - 28.3\n      - Moderate\n    - - c_n = 1.50 (mvp_002/003)\n      - 0.31\n      - 0.65\n      - 43.8\n      - Large\n  - name: Synthetic c_n Recovery at Real-Data Magnitude\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - c_n=0.15\n    - c_n=0.50\n    - c_n=1.50\n    rows:\n    - - c_n RMSE\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.28\n      - 0.18\n    - - 95% CI coverage\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.87\n      - 0.91\n    - - c_n correlation (true vs fitted)\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.72\n      - 0.92\n    - - Pct models correctly ranked by c_n\n      - 0.55\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.94\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bias magnitude surface plot: x=c_n level, y=rho(d,c·e), z=theta bias — steep\n    only in high-c_n, high-rho corner'\n  - 'Confounding scatter at 4 c_n levels: panel plot showing scatter tightening to\n    diagonal as c_n decreases'\n  analysis: 'The formal observation remains a valid theoretical contribution — OVB\n    exists regardless of magnitude. But the practical punchline under worst tier:\n    the bias is negligible (0.02 logits) because contamination is too low to create\n    meaningful confounding. The synthetic c_n recovery at real-data magnitude (correlation\n    0.35) confirms G-MFRM cannot reliably rank models by contamination susceptibility\n    when the signal is this weak. Pivot framing: ''we derive the confounding mechanism\n    and show it activates under conditions X; current benchmarks happen to NOT meet\n    these conditions; the correction is available when needed.'''\n- name: wildbench_replication\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: Replicate G-MFRM pipeline on WildBench as third benchmark for cross-benchmark\n    generalization\n  expected_results:\n    wildbench_n_items: 1024\n    wildbench_n_judges: 6\n    wildbench_kendall_tau_gmfrm: 0.74\n    wildbench_kendall_tau_mfrm: 0.75\n    wildbench_kendall_tau_uda: 0.76\n    wildbench_delta_tau_gmfrm_vs_best: -0.02\n    wildbench_cn_median: 0.09\n    wildbench_cn_max: 0.38\n    wildbench_ei_median: 0.08\n    wildbench_convergence_rhat_max: 1.14\n    wildbench_convergence_ess_min: 41\n    cross_benchmark_cn_correlation: 0.28\n    cross_benchmark_theta_correlation: 0.92\n  result_rationale: WildBench uses more diverse, harder items sourced from real user\n    interactions — these items are LESS likely to be contaminated than MT-Bench/Arena-Hard\n    because they're more recent and less standardized. Under worst tier, WildBench\n    shows even lower contamination (median c_n=0.09) than the other benchmarks. G-MFRM\n    slightly underperforms both MFRM-only and UDA because the contamination parameters\n    add noise. Cross-benchmark c_n correlation is weak (r=0.28) — models don't show\n    consistent contamination patterns across benchmarks, suggesting what little contamination\n    exists is benchmark-specific (item-level memorization) rather than model-level\n    training data overlap. Cross-benchmark θ correlation (0.92) confirms ability estimates\n    are stable regardless of contamination correction method.\n  tables:\n  - name: Cross-Benchmark Comparison\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - tau_GMFRM\n    - tau_MFRM\n    - tau_UDA\n    - cn_median\n    - ei_median\n    rows:\n    - - MT-Bench\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.17\n      - 0.14\n    - - Arena-Hard\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.14\n      - 0.11\n    - - WildBench\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.09\n      - 0.08\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Cross-benchmark c_n scatter matrix: 3 panels showing weak correlations between\n    benchmarks — contamination is benchmark-specific, not model-intrinsic'\n  analysis: 'WildBench replication confirms worst-tier pattern generalizes: negligible\n    contamination, no ranking benefit from G-MFRM. The low cross-benchmark c_n correlation\n    (0.28) suggests that contamination — what little exists — is item-specific rather\n    than reflecting stable model-level susceptibility. This weakens the c_n construct\n    validity further but supports the ''benchmarks are clean'' narrative.'\n- name: adversarial_robustness\n  plan_id: exp-011\n  description: Test G-MFRM robustness against adversarial contamination following\n    Wang et al. (ICLR 2026)\n  expected_results:\n    adversarial_injection_levels:\n    - 0.0\n    - 0.3\n    - 0.5\n    - 1.0\n    - 1.5\n    tau_gmfrm_at_0.0: 0.77\n    tau_gmfrm_at_0.3: 0.76\n    tau_gmfrm_at_0.5: 0.78\n    tau_gmfrm_at_1.0: 0.82\n    tau_gmfrm_at_1.5: 0.87\n    tau_sequential_at_1.5: 0.79\n    tau_mfrm_at_1.5: 0.75\n    crossover_point_cn: 0.7\n    cn_recovery_at_injection_1.5: 0.81\n    detection_power_at_real_cn: 0.15\n    detection_power_at_injected_1.5: 0.92\n  result_rationale: Under worst tier, natural contamination is negligible — but we\n    can INJECT synthetic contamination to demonstrate G-MFRM works when contamination\n    IS present. By artificially inflating scores for specific models on high-e_i items,\n    we create a controlled setting. At injection level 1.5 (matching mvp_002/003),\n    G-MFRM recovers well (τ=0.87 vs sequential's 0.79). The crossover point where\n    G-MFRM starts outperforming is c_n≈0.7 — above real-data levels but demonstrating\n    the method's potential. Detection power at real c_n levels is only 0.15 (cannot\n    detect what isn't there), but jumps to 0.92 at injected 1.5. This experiment redeems\n    the method theoretically while confirming it's unnecessary on current benchmarks.\n  tables:\n  - name: Adversarial Injection Results\n    columns:\n    - Injection_level\n    - tau_GMFRM\n    - tau_Sequential\n    - tau_MFRM\n    - cn_recovery_corr\n    - Detection_power\n    rows:\n    - - 0.0\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.35\n      - 0.15\n    - - 0.3\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.42\n      - 0.25\n    - - 0.5\n      - 0.78\n      - 0.77\n      - 0.76\n      - 0.58\n      - 0.45\n    - - 1.0\n      - 0.82\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.74\n      - 0.78\n    - - 1.5\n      - 0.87\n      - 0.79\n      - 0.75\n      - 0.81\n      - 0.92\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Crossover plot: G-MFRM vs baselines as function of injected contamination level\n    — lines cross around c_n=0.7, with G-MFRM pulling ahead in high-contamination\n    regime'\n  - Annotated vertical line at 'real data level' (c_n≈0.15) showing all methods equivalent\n    there\n  analysis: 'Adversarial robustness provides the strongest pivot evidence: G-MFRM\n    works when contamination exists, it just doesn''t exist on current benchmarks.\n    The crossover at c_n=0.7 gives a principled threshold for when G-MFRM becomes\n    necessary. Framing: ''G-MFRM is an insurance policy — unnecessary today but protective\n    against future contamination increases as training data grows.'' Also validates\n    the methodology for historical benchmarks (e.g., MMLU original) where contamination\n    IS known to be high.'\n","numLines":1092,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1092}}}
